WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.040
In 1963, an anthropologist called
Edward T. Hall

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.450
realised there was no term for the study of
personal space,

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.200
so he invented one.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.770
Because that's the sort of thing that anthropologists
like to do.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.260
He called it ‘proxemics’, and it caught
on,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.360
because it turned out to be a good way to
let people think about abstract questions

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.360
like:

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.380
how do you react when someone gets too close,
too fast?

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.790
What we have here is a robotic spider dress.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.710
So it’s a dress that has mechanical limbs
that are reacting.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:35.820
It is reacting on you coming closer, so you
coming near.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.010
There are two proximity sensors here.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.150
They are in the balls of the design.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.740
They are sensing when you come too close.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.510
There are 12 states that are pre-programmed.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.660
Basically, it’s deciding between 1 of those
12 states

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.800
in order to create one of these animations.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.489
In his book, Hall proposes four distance zones:

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:59.551
the public, the social, the personal and the
intimate.

00:00:59.551 --> 00:01:04.820
Now, in the sort of life coaching seminars
that people get sent to,

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.510
and in middle management textbooks,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.979
those zones are treated as gospel,

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:09.930
as 'this is how everyone behaves'.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.500
But in Hall’s own words, the zones were
only a first approximation.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.490
They were based on a tiny sample of university
educated Americans.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.230
They weren’t representative of human behaviour
in general,

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.290
and they weren’t meant to be.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.350
They were crude stereotypes.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.930
Actually, a lot of the book is crude stereotypes.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.450
There’s a whole section just titled ‘The
French’,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.790
which describes them as ‘sensually involved
with each other.’

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.040
I used 3D modelling in order to create this
design.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.890
I'm using a software called ‘Maya’, and
then we 3D printed it out.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.960
It is 3D in nylon, in a technique called ‘selective
laser sintering.’

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:51.390
You can see here a lot of difficult geometries
that are very thin at places,

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:52.650
and they are very delicate.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.640
The technique of selective laser sintering
means that you have

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.229
basically a big box of powder, which is, in
this case, nylon,

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:00.420
and you have your laser beam.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.420
It’s sintering out,

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.790
it’s materialising exactly the model you
created.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.910
Basically, when you take this design out,

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.709
you're taking it from a big basket of powder,

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:11.359
and all the powder is falling off.

00:02:11.359 --> 00:02:13.489
The proximity sensors are in this dress,

00:02:13.489 --> 00:02:14.819
so they're in the front of the dress

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:19.299
and they are sensing in a beam that's being
sent to the microcontroller

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:20.650
which is in the back piece.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.579
As a scientific theory, the field of proxemics
is vague.

00:02:24.579 --> 00:02:27.450
Hall just sort of thought it up and stated
it in his book.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.010
There aren’t many studies backed up with
rigorous examination and statistics.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.549
It’s mostly a set of observations and ideas.

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:39.029
But despite what some hyper-rationalist people
might think, those have value too.

00:02:39.029 --> 00:02:41.170
Being aware of the hidden rules that guide
us,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.419
asking why we act the way we do:

00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:47.939
that can be a powerful tool for figuring out
the way that the world works.

00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:50.319
And visualising something that isn't just
invisible,

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:52.329
but is actually an abstract concept?

00:02:52.329 --> 00:02:56.140
That can be a really powerful way to help
understand yourself.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.760
Thanks to Anouk Wipprecht!

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:58.760
Her dress is on display

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.080
at the Autodesk Gallery in San Francisco until
March 2017.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.299
Pull down the description for more links

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:05.139
about her, the dress and the gallery.

